OpenROAD PR 10662 -- rsz: BufferToInverters move -- rebased onto master.

Carried here so the study's "as submitted" arm is reproducible from this
repository rather than from a fork that may be rebased or deleted. The
change is erendn's; the rebase is ours.

  https://github.com/The-OpenROAD-Project/OpenROAD/pull/10662
  merge base 14b1ef13293b2a4c165d67cd81b4c48a4be9c5f4  (2026-06-12)
  head       0c49c2bd5ace59b8048373343c66f70669941bea  (2026-06-17)
  rebased onto 63fe72c577, the @openroad pin, which is also master

REBASED, NOT VERBATIM, AND WHY THAT IS THE POINT

The pull request's five commits were squashed and replayed onto the pin.
A QoR number is only worth reading against the tree a maintainer would
merge into: measured against a June merge base it answers a question
nobody is asking, because 2364 commits of resizer work have landed since.
So the thing under test is the rebase, and the rebase is part of what the
study delivers -- a pull request that waits three months for a decision
acquires a rebase cost that did not exist when it was opened, and someone
has to pay it before any number can be produced.

The replay was clean: 27 files, +732/-7, the same shape as the original,
and every rsz API the move depends on -- GeneratorContext,
hasBlockingBufferRemovalMove, canRemoveBuffer, cellDriveResistance,
rejectedMove, run_config_.match_cell_footprint -- still exists on the pin.
A clean replay is not a working build, so the build is what proves it.

The move is not in the default sequence: it runs only when
"buffer_to_inverters" is named in repair_timing -sequence, which is what
SETUP_MOVE_SEQUENCE feeds through ORFS's repair_timing_helper. No ORFS
patch is needed to reach it; patches/0050 is carried for the fork seam,
which is a different concern.

Do not edit the diff below to make a result come out -- an edited copy
stops being the thing under test. Corrections that came out of the pull
request's own review live in a separate patch, on top of this one.

Retire when 10662 merges or is closed upstream.

